## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests robustness of an event-driven payments pipeline, focusing on idempotency, end-to-end correctness, and resilience under partial failures. It mirrors real-world fintech and high‑throughput marketplaces where retries and outbox patterns are essential.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and exactly-once semantics in asynchronous flows
- Outbox pattern, event bus (Kafka), and compensating actions
- Fault injection, timeouts, retries, and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: spawn N concurrent requests with varying idempotency keys
const axios=require('axios');
async function send(batch) {
  const requests=batch.map((i)=>axios.post('/payments/process', {
    amount:100, currency:'USD', paymentMethod:'card', idempotencyKey:`k-${i}`
  }));
  return Promise.all(requests);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure idempotency effectiveness under network partitions?
- How would you simulate non-deterministic outbox flush delays across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests reliability of composite APIs with dependent services.

## Key Concepts
- Downstream latency, timeouts, partial failures, circuit breakers
- Idempotent retries, exponential backoff, observability
- End-to-end timing and schema validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test using jest + nock
const nock = require('nock');
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');
test('partial data when one downstream times out', async () => {
  nock('http://sales').get('/daily').delay(3000).reply(200, {sales: 1000});
  nock('http://customers').get('/daily').reply(200, {customers: 50});
  const res = await request(app).get('/reports/daily');
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(200);
  expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('sales');
  expect(res.headers).toHaveProperty('x-downstream-warn');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under sustained latency?
- What metrics would you validate in your observability stack?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates advanced API testing knowledge, specifically multitenant isolation, schema versioning, canary deployments, and production-like failure scenarios. It also probes understanding of contract testing discipline and observable latency behavior under burst traffic conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Contract testing with Pact between ingestion and downstream processing services
- Per-tenant isolation and data residency guarantees across test scenarios
- Schema versioning strategies with backward compatibility validation
- Feature-flag-driven canary rollouts with quantitative acceptance criteria
- Latency targets (p95/p99) under burst load and SLA alignment
- Idempotency guarantees and DLQ/resilience patterns for downstream outages

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pact contract test: consumer side (ingest service)
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');
const { like, eachLike } = require('pact-matchers');

const provider = new Pact({
  consumer: 'anomaly-detect-api',
  provider: 'downstream-processor',
  port: 1234
});

before(() => provider.setup());
after(() => provider.finalize());

describe('POST /ingest contract', () => {
  it('validates anomaly payload schema', async () => {
    await provider.addInteraction({
      state: 'tenant abc-123 exists',
      uponReceiving: 'a valid anomaly ingest request',
      withRequest: {
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/ingest',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Tenant-ID': 'abc-123' },
        body: { metrics: eachLike({ timestamp: 1700000000, value: 42.5 }) }
      },
      willRespondWith: {
        status: 202,
        body: like({ ingestionId: 'uuid-here' })
      }
    });
  });
});
```

